<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<speak version="1.1" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-GB">
<!-- The Watch Below, Chapter 14: The Younger Son. Point of view: Calder. -->
<p><s>Chapter Fourteen.</s></p><break time="800ms"/>
<p><s>The Younger Son.</s></p><break time="1800ms"/>
<!-- p1 · reflective -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">The Clan says that a ford remembers everyone who crosses it, and how heavy they were. That’s why the stones in a ford are smooth.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p2 -->
<p>The ford on Hollow Beck was old. Older than the hold. Older than the Clan. The Drover’s Way came down off the moor to it in a long slant and went up the other side in another, and in between, the beck ran wide and shallow over a bed of smooth brown stones, with alders leaning over it on both banks and a shepherd’s shieling of turf and stone on the slope above.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p3 -->
<p><sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s grandfather and his brothers had settled their quarrels there, when they were young, before anybody was chief of anything. <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> had told <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> that. He’d been proud of it.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p4 -->
<p>They left Harrow’s End at two in the morning, in the dark, <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> and Hob, in Hob’s cart, with the mended axle groaning and Hob’s old cob in the shafts. Old Garrow had stood at his door in his nightshirt to see them off and said his knees were very sorry, and <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> had believed him, and Hob hadn’t.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p5 · humour · Hob -->
<p>“Knees,” Hob said, for the first league. “Knees. He’ll be telling everyone at the Wheel next week he’d have come if it weren’t for his knees. He’ll make a song of it.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p6 · Calder -->
<p>“His knees are bad.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p7 · humour · Hob -->
<p>“Everybody’s knees are bad. Mine are bad. I’m here.” Hob flapped the reins at the cob. “I’m here because somebody’s got to witness properly. You’ll stand there like a gatepost and not say anything, and then when they ask you what happened you’ll say <emphasis level="moderate">they shook hands</emphasis>, and that’s all anyone’ll get out of you. Somebody’s got to notice who shook first, and how long, and what their faces were doing. That’s witnessing.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p8 · Calder -->
<p>“<sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> didn’t ask you to notice their faces.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p9 · humour · Hob -->
<p>“<sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>,” said Hob, “doesn’t know what’s good for him.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p10 -->
<p>The sun came up as they climbed. It was a clear day, cold, with frost in the hollows and a high thin sky, and the whole moor went gold and then brown as the light came on it. Geese went over in long lines, going south. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> sat beside Hob on the board with his hands in his coat pockets and the chain in his right hand, and watched the hills come up toward them.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p11 · reflective · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He’d slept badly. He’d dreamed about the man on the bucket again. He’d woken up in the dark and lain there, and thought: <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">after. When it’s done.</prosody> And then he’d got up and dressed and come out to the gate where Hob was waiting, and the feeling of having decided had been there still, warm, like a coal in his chest. It was there now. He held the chain in his pocket and felt it.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p12 · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">After.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p13 -->
<p>They came over the last rise above the ford in the middle of the morning, an hour before noon, and saw it below them: the long slant of the Way going down to the water, the beck shining, the alders, the shieling on the slope with its turf roof. Nobody there yet. The banks empty. The water going over the brown stones the way it had for a thousand years.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p14 · Hob -->
<p>“We’re first,” said Hob, pleased. “Good. We’ll get a good place.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p15 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> looked down at the ford. He couldn’t have said what was wrong. Something about the way it lay there, empty, in the clear cold light. The way the water moved.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p16 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">There was a horse grazing on the far bank, under the alders. A tall bay. Saddled. Reins trailing.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p17 · Calder -->
<p>“Hob,” he said.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p18 · Hob -->
<p>“What?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p19 · Calder -->
<p>“Stop the cart.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p20 -->
<p>Hob stopped the cart. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> was already down. He went down the slope of the Way on foot, not running, not yet, as you’d walk toward a frightened animal: steady, near, not straight at it. He watched the bay. The bay lifted its head and looked at him and went back to grazing. Its saddle was good leather, Clan work, with a grey cloth under it, and a black hound stitched on the cloth.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p21 · grief -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">He knew that horse. He’d held its head at the gate of his own top field, four nights ago, while its rider leaned from the saddle and told him to tell Wren she danced better than any girl at the hold.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p22 · urgent -->
<p><prosody rate="105%">Then he was running.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p23 -->
<p><sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> was in the water.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p24 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">He was at the edge of the ford, on the near side, where the beck came over the stones in a long shallow sheet and the alders leaned down close. He was lying on his face with his arms out, half in the water and half out of it, as if he’d been going to drink and had lain down instead. The water went over his back and round his shoulders and out past his hands, and it was red.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p25 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> went into the beck to his knees and turned him over.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p26 · grief -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">He’d never done anything like it before. Afterwards he couldn’t remember deciding to. He just found that he had his arms under <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s shoulders and was lifting him, and <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> was very heavy and very cold and his head went back over <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>’s arm, and his face was the same face, long and thin and too young for itself, with the eyes open and the mouth a little open as if he’d been about to say something cheerful. There was water in his hair. There was a wound in his side, low, under the ribs, and another under the collarbone, and his shirt was black with it.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p27 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> dragged him up out of the beck onto the stones of the bank and knelt beside him and didn’t know what to do.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p28 · grief: quiet, stunned · Calder -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">“<sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>,” he said.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p29 -->
<p>It was a stupid thing to say. He said it anyway.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p30 · grief · Hob, whispered, over and over -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">Behind him, up the slope, he heard Hob shout something, high and cracked, and then the cart rattling, and then Hob’s voice again, nearer, saying <prosody volume="-5dB">oh gods oh gods oh gods</prosody> over and over in a whisper, like a prayer that didn’t know any other words.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p31 -->
<p>He made himself look.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p32 -->
<p>He didn’t want to. He wanted to close <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s eyes and put his coat over him and walk away up the hill and keep walking, and never come back to this ford, or any ford, ever. He wanted to be at home in the byre with Bramble leaning on him. But he’d been asked to be a witness, and a witness looks.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p33 -->
<p>So he looked.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p34 -->
<p><sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s knife was in its sheath on his belt. The good knife, the one with the hound on the pommel. Still in its sheath. He hadn’t drawn it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p35 -->
<p>His sword was on his saddle, on the bay, on the far bank. He hadn’t drawn that either.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p36 -->
<p>The wounds were from close in, made by a blade, and not by an arrow or a spear from the bank. Someone had come right up to him, close enough to touch, close enough to talk to, and he hadn’t drawn his knife, because he hadn’t thought he needed to.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p37 · dread · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="96%"><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">Someone he let come close.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p38 -->
<p>There was something caught on the alders above the water, at the height of a man’s shoulder, where a branch hung low over the bank. A strip of grey cloth. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> stood up and reached for it, and it came away in his hand: an armband, plain grey wool, the mourning band every man in the Clan had been wearing since Haldor’s pyre. Plain grey. No thread of red in it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p39 -->
<p>East-side grey. <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s men’s grey.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p40 -->
<p>In the shallows, a little way out, half under the water where it ran fast over the stones, lay an axe. A Clan axe, long-hafted, with a bearded blade, the kind every man at <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s table carried at his belt.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p41 · tension -->
<p><prosody rate="97%"><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> stood on the bank with the armband in one hand and his other hand in his coat pocket, closed so hard round the chain that the links bit into his palm.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p42 · grief · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%"><prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">After,</prosody> he thought. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">When it’s done. When it’s safe.</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p43 · Teo (remembered) -->
<p>He thought of a man on a bucket by a stable door, three paces from the foot of the cliff path, mending a halter that never got mended. He thought of <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> turning round on the last step, lit up, saying it out loud. <prosody volume="-3dB">Four days after your fair, Fenn. Noon. The ford on Hollow Beck.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p44 -->
<p>He thought: no. No. I don’t know that. I don’t know anything. It’s an armband and an axe, and they’re <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s. That’s what’s here. That’s what a witness sees.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p45 -->
<p>He stood on the bank and held the chain in his pocket and didn’t take it out.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p46 -->
<p>Arn came at noon, with his six.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p47 -->
<p>They came over the far rise at a canter, six riders in grey with the red thread in their armbands, and Arn at the front on a black horse, and they saw the bay grazing under the alders and they saw the cart on the near slope and they saw Hob, white-faced, on his knees by the water, and they saw <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> standing on the bank. And then they saw what was lying on the stones at <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>’s feet.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p48 · grief: silent, terrible -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">Arn didn’t make a sound. That was the worst of it. He didn’t shout or cry out. He came down the far slope and through the ford at a gallop, with the water going up white on both sides of the black horse, and threw himself off it on the near bank before it had stopped, and went down on his knees beside his brother, and put his hands on him.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p49 -->
<p>He stayed like that. His six sat their horses in the ford behind him, in the water, not moving. The beck went over the stones.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p50 -->
<p>At last Arn looked up at <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p51 · dread -->
<p><prosody rate="96%">His face was white, and perfectly still, and there was nothing in it at all, no grief and no rage, like the black water at <sub alias="Cold-tarn">Coldtarn</sub>.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p52 · Arn -->
<p>“You,” he said. “The farmer. The barley man. The witness.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p53 · Calder -->
<p>“Yes.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p54 · Arn -->
<p>“What did you see?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p55 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> looked at him. He looked at <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s face, with the open eyes. He held out the armband.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p56 · Calder -->
<p>“He was dead when I got here,” he said. “In the water. His knife’s still in its sheath. This was on the alders. There’s an axe in the shallows.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p57 -->
<p>That was all true. Every word.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p58 -->
<p>Arn took the armband. He looked at it. Plain grey. No thread of red. He turned it over in his hands as if there might be something written on the inside. He looked at the axe in the shallows, and one of his six got down and waded out and fetched it, and brought it, dripping, and held it out, and Arn didn’t take it. He just looked.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p59 · Arn -->
<p>“His knife’s in its sheath,” he said.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p60 · Calder -->
<p>“Yes.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p61 · grief: very quiet · Arn, very quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="94%"><prosody volume="-5dB">“He didn’t draw.”</prosody> Arn’s voice was very quiet. <prosody volume="-5dB">“He wouldn’t, would he. Not against family. Not against Uncle’s men. Not at the ford, not today, not with his hands out.”</prosody> He looked down at his brother. <prosody volume="-5dB">“He came early. He always came early. He’d have been standing here in the water at first light, waiting, so he could be the first thing they saw. Smiling. And they came down the Way and he walked up to meet them with his hands out, and they…”</prosody></prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p62 -->
<p>He stopped.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p63 -->
<p>He put his hand flat on his brother’s chest, on the black soaked shirt, and held it there. When he lifted it, the palm was red.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p64 -->
<p>He stood up. He held up the red hand, so his six could see it, so <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> could see it, so the ford and the alders and the hills could see it.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p65 · anger: quiet, cold oath · Arn, quite quietly; Arn -->
<p><prosody volume="-5dB">“By my father in <sub alias="Cold-tarn">Coldtarn</sub>,”</prosody> said Arn <sub alias="Var">Varr</sub>, quite quietly, <prosody volume="-5dB">“and my brother on these stones, and every chief of the <sub alias="Var">Varr</sub> who went into the water before them. I’ll have the hand that did this. And the arm it hangs from. And the man the arm belongs to. And every man who eats at his table and every man who wears his grey. I’ll have them all. There’ll be no peace at this ford or any ford, there’ll be no levy and no fair and no bread, until I’ve put <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> <sub alias="Var">Varr</sub> into the water at <sub alias="Cold-tarn">Coldtarn</sub> with my own hands, and all his men after him, one by one, and stood on the black stone and watched them sink.”</prosody> He closed the red hand into a fist. “I swear it. Witness.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p66 -->
<p>He was looking at <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p67 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> couldn’t have said anything if the hills had fallen. He stood on the bank with the chain in his pocket and the beck going by over the stones, and was a witness.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p68 -->
<p><sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> came a quarter of an hour later, with his six, up the Way from the Ram’s Head, where he’d lain the night. They came by the near rise behind the cart, the one <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> and Hob had come over, where the long slant of the Way began.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p69 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> heard them before he saw them. He looked up and saw them come over the top, six big men in plain grey with axes at their belts, and <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> at the front on a huge grey horse, and he saw <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> see it. The bay. The black horse. Arn’s six in a line along the bank with their swords out now. Arn standing over something on the stones.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p70 -->
<p><sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> stopped his horse at the top of the slope and sat there.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p71 -->
<p>At last he came down the Way at a walk, alone, leaving his six at the top, and stopped at the edge of the ford on the near side, and looked down from the saddle at his nephew lying on the stones.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p72 -->
<p><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> was close enough to see his face. He watched it, because a witness watches.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p73 · grief -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">He would think about that face for a long time afterwards. He would turn it over in the dark, as you turn over a coin you think might be false. It was a big, heavy, closed face, the face of a man who had spent fifty years making sure nobody ever knew what he was thinking. For one moment, looking down at <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> on the stones, it opened. Just for a moment. And what was in it was grief, plain and enormous and real, like a man who has been struck from behind and doesn’t yet know how badly.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p74 -->
<p>Then it closed again.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p75 · Ulric -->
<p>“Who did this?” said <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p76 -->
<p>Arn held up the armband.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p77 -->
<p><sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> looked at it. He looked at the axe, dripping in the hands of Arn’s man. He looked at his own six, at the top of the slope, in their plain grey, with their axes at their belts.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p78 · Ulric -->
<p>“No,” he said.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p79 · Arn -->
<p>“Your grey,” said Arn. “Your axe. Your men, the only ones who knew the hour, coming down the Way at noon. And his knife in its sheath, because he walked up to meet them with his hands out.” He took a step forward. ”<emphasis level="moderate">No?</emphasis>”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p80 · Ulric, deep, slow, steady; Ulric -->
<p><prosody volume="-5dB">“I didn’t do this.”</prosody> <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s voice was deep and slow and quite steady. <prosody volume="-5dB">“I came here to shake your hand, boy. In the water. Because he asked me to.”</prosody> He looked down at <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> again. “Because he was the only one of you I’d have done it for.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p81 · Arn -->
<p>“Liar.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p82 · anger · Ulric -->
<p>“Think, Arn. Think, for once in your life. If I wanted him dead, would I leave my grey on a bush and my axe in the water, like a signpost? Would I kill him at the ford I agreed to, on the day I agreed to, an hour before I was due to be here? I’m not a fool.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p83 · anger · Arn -->
<p>“No,” said Arn. “You’re not a fool. You’re a man who’s been waiting thirty years for a chair. And now there’s nobody left between you and it but me.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p84 -->
<p>He drew his sword.</p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p85 -->
<p>It didn’t last long.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p86 · action -->
<p><prosody rate="104%">One of Arn’s six went past Arn, at the gallop, through the ford toward the slope where <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s six were, with his sword up and a shout coming out of him that wasn’t a word. One of <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s men came down to meet him. They met at the bottom of the slope, at the edge of the water, and the horses crashed together, and there was a sound like an axe going into a log, and then one of them was on the ground, and it was <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s man, and he wasn’t moving.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p87 -->
<p>Somebody blew a horn. A long, low, rising note, held, and then cut off.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p88 -->
<p>From the top of the slope, <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s men answered it with another.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p89 · action -->
<p><prosody rate="104%">Then everyone was moving. <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s six came down the slope and Arn’s six went up to meet them, and <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub> himself was turning his huge grey horse and roaring something, and Arn was in the saddle again, and Hob had <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> by the coat and was dragging him, up the bank, away from the water, toward the shieling, shouting in his ear words he couldn’t hear. Behind them there was the sound of horses in water and iron on iron and men shouting.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p90 -->
<p>They got behind the shieling wall and crouched there. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> found he still had the armband in his hand. He didn’t remember Arn giving it back.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p91 · action -->
<p><prosody rate="104%">Somebody had set the gorse on fire. He didn’t see who. He smelled it first, the sharp hot smell of gorse going up, and then he saw it: a line of fire running across the slope above the ford in the dry brown bushes, crackling, throwing up thick white smoke. The turf roof of the shieling caught next, above their heads, and they had to run again, Hob dragging him, down the slope behind the shieling into a hollow full of bracken, where they lay on their faces in the cold wet fronds and listened.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p92 -->
<p>It went on for a while. Then the horns blew again, both of them, and the sounds changed, and drew apart, and went away: <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s men away east over the moor, Arn’s up the Way toward the hold. Shouting, fading. Horses, fading.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p93 · grief: quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">There was only the fire crackling in the gorse, and the beck going over the stones, and Hob crying quietly beside him in the bracken, with his face in his arm.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p94 -->
<p>When they came back down, the ford was empty except for the dead.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p95 -->
<p><sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s man, at the bottom of the slope. One of Arn’s, further up, who <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> hadn’t seen fall. <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>, on the stones at the water’s edge, where <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> had left him, with his face to the sky and his eyes open and the water going by beside him as if nothing had happened.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p96 -->
<p>Arn had left him. Or forgotten him. Or not been able to stay.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p97 -->
<p>The smoke from the gorse and the shieling went up over the hill in a great grey column, three columns, four, spreading out at the top.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p98 · grief: tender, slow -->
<p><prosody rate="94%"><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> knelt beside <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> and closed his eyes. It took two tries; the first time, they wouldn’t stay. He took off his coat, with the chain in its pocket, and laid it over <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> from the chest up, so his face was covered, and sat down on the stones beside him, with his arms round his knees, and waited, because somebody from the hold would come for him, and somebody should be here when they did.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p99 -->
<p>Hob sat down on the other side.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p100 · Hob, ragged -->
<p>“You saw,” said Hob, after a while. His voice was ragged. “The armband. The axe. You saw. <sub alias="Ullrick">Ulric</sub>’s men.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p101 · Calder -->
<p>“I saw an armband and an axe.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p102 · Hob -->
<p>“That’s what I said.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p103 · Calder -->
<p>“No,” said <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub>. “It isn’t.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p104 · grief · Calder (thought) -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">Hob looked at him. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> didn’t look back. He sat on the stones with his coat over <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s face and the chain in the coat, and looked at the water, and thought about a man on a bucket, and a knife with a ring of six dots on the pommel, and a promise. <prosody rate="97%" volume="-4dB">After. When it’s done.</prosody> He’d held it in his hand all morning. He’d held it in his hand when he turned <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> over in the water.</prosody></p><break time="800ms"/>
<!-- p105 · grief -->
<p><prosody rate="94%">It was done now. There was no after.</prosody></p>
<break time="1600ms"/><!-- scene break -->
<!-- p106 -->
<p>His own six came down from the hold in the afternoon, at the hour he’d given them, too late. They took him up the Way at dusk on a hurdle, walking beside it, with his bay behind and the reins over the saddle. <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> and Hob went home in the cart in the dark without speaking, the whole long way down the Way, with the cob picking its own path by the starlight.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p107 -->
<p>It was the small hours when they came down off the moor. At the bottom, where the Way came down to the river lane above Harrow’s End, there were still lights at the <sub alias="Eel-weer">Eelweir</sub>: lanterns on the jetty, and men standing about. Hob stopped the cart without being asked.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p108 · Hob, calling out -->
<p>“The ford,” Hob called. “Have you heard? Have you heard about the ford?”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p109 -->
<p>They’d heard. The whole river had heard. The horns had carried. The smoke had been seen from Greyhold. Old Fisk himself came up to the cart with a lantern and stood there with his old face working.</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p110 · Old Fisk -->
<p>“<sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub> <sub alias="Var">Varr</sub>,” he said. “The young one. The good one.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p111 · Calder -->
<p>“Yes.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p112 · Old Fisk -->
<p>“Gods. Gods. That’s war, then. That’s war, now, for certain.” Fisk shook his head. Then, because he was a fisherman, and fishermen always have to say the other thing, the thing that’s on their minds, he said: “And that Greyhold boat’s still on the island. Four days, now. My boys saw it from the punt. Pulled up on the spit under a log. Nobody’s seen anybody. Nobody’s come off.” He looked out across the water into the dark. “Whoever went on there, they’re still there. Or they’re not. Either way, it’s a bad week for it.”</p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p113 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%"><sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> sat on the cart in his shirtsleeves, because his coat was up at the hold over <sub alias="Tayo">Teo</sub>’s face, and the chain with it, and he was very cold, and he hadn’t noticed until now.</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p114 · tender · Old Fisk, gently -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">“Go home, <sub alias="Cawlder">Calder</sub> Fenn,” said Fisk gently. “You look like death. Go home to your girl.”</prosody></p><break time="500ms"/>
<!-- p115 · quiet -->
<p><prosody rate="95%">He went home to his girl.</prosody></p>
<break time="1800ms"/>
</speak>
